WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Marcus and Elena return for a full family review.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:09.533
In this new exercise, their stated survivor income shortfall is thirty-five

3
00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:13.160
thousand dollars per year for fifteen years.

4
00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:16.420
Assume no inflation or investment return.

5
00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.760
That creates five hundred twenty-five thousand dollars of income need.

6
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.200
They also identify two hundred thousand dollars of separate immediate

7
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.360
obligations, with no overlap between the categories.

8
00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:31.720
The total is seven hundred twenty-five thousand dollars.

9
00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:36.440
They have one hundred twenty-five thousand dollars of resources explicitly

10
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.040
available for these needs.

11
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.000
Subtracting those resources leaves a six hundred thousand dollar illustrative

12
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.440
gap. This is not a quote or a personal recommendation.

13
00:00:47.178 --> 00:00:50.378
The family expects a large income need while the children are

14
00:00:50.378 --> 00:00:54.618
dependent, but also wants to understand smaller lasting obligations.

15
00:00:55.558 --> 00:00:59.058
A level term policy can provide a stated death benefit during

16
00:00:59.058 --> 00:01:03.258
its specified term. Coverage after that period depends on the actual

17
00:01:03.258 --> 00:01:07.398
renewal or conversion rights, deadlines and premiums.

18
00:01:08.478 --> 00:01:12.458
Whole life uses a different structure, with permanent protection and

19
00:01:12.458 --> 00:01:15.978
contractual cash values when its required conditions are maintained.

20
00:01:17.178 --> 00:01:22.198
Compare the actual benefits, guarantees, funding and

21
00:01:22.198 --> 00:01:27.138
affordable duration. If existing coverage might be replaced, follow

22
00:01:27.138 --> 00:01:30.938
Maryland's replacement process and assess lost benefits and costs before

23
00:01:30.938 --> 00:01:33.178
asking the family to end that policy.

24
00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.440
In the proposed arrangement, Marcus owns a policy on his own

25
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:42.340
life, and Elena is the primary beneficiary.

26
00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:47.780
Marcus holds the ownership rights and is also the insured person,

27
00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.480
but those are two separate roles.

28
00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.960
Elena's beneficiary designation does not make her life insured by that

29
00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:00.980
base policy. A contingent beneficiary is considered when the primary

30
00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:05.340
cannot take the benefit under the applicable contract and legal facts.

31
00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:09.360
The family also wants funds available for their minor children.

32
00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:15.080
Naming a child does not automatically authorize unrestricted direct payment

33
00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:19.600
to the child. A properly arranged trust or other lawful method

34
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:24.100
requires attention to the actual designation and legal requirements.

35
00:02:24.822 --> 00:02:29.322
Marcus completes the application and gives the initial premium to the

36
00:02:29.322 --> 00:02:34.462
producer. Those acts do not by themselves prove that every claim

37
00:02:34.462 --> 00:02:39.282
is covered immediately. Read the receipt and its actual coverage conditions,

38
00:02:40.262 --> 00:02:44.462
the insurer's underwriting decision, and any delivery requirements.

39
00:02:45.522 --> 00:02:49.822
The producer must not invent authority to waive a condition or

40
00:02:49.822 --> 00:02:51.522
alter a contract promise.

41
00:02:51.522 --> 00:02:56.002
Application answers should be complete and truthful,

42
00:02:56.922 --> 00:02:59.122
and corrections should be handled properly.

43
00:02:59.962 --> 00:03:04.842
If Marcus's health changes before delivery, follow the insurer's required

44
00:03:04.842 --> 00:03:07.902
process instead of hiding that change.

45
00:03:08.702 --> 00:03:11.962
A good-health statement can be part of the delivery requirements.

46
00:03:12.822 --> 00:03:16.662
The effective-date question is answered by the relevant documents and facts,

47
00:03:17.202 --> 00:03:20.842
not by assuming that a handshake created unconditional protection.

48
00:03:21.528 --> 00:03:23.228
Later, a premium is missed.

49
00:03:23.848 --> 00:03:28.108
Before describing the policy as lapsed, identify the due date and

50
00:03:28.108 --> 00:03:29.488
the applicable grace period.

51
00:03:30.008 --> 00:03:34.448
For standard Maryland individual life coverage within the cited rule, the

52
00:03:34.448 --> 00:03:37.988
grace provision is one month of at least thirty days, with

53
00:03:37.988 --> 00:03:40.228
the policy continuing during that period.

54
00:03:41.148 --> 00:03:43.268
The contract may provide a longer period.

55
00:03:43.888 --> 00:03:46.720
That is different from a free-look period
after delivery,

56
00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:51.580
a reinstatement opportunity after lapse
or an incontestability period.

57
00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:54.550
If a covered death occurs during grace,

58
00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.620
the unpaid premium may be deducted as allowed.

59
00:03:58.148 --> 00:04:01.420
Do not tell the family that every missed payment ends protection

60
00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:05.108
immediately, or that reinstatement is automatic without meeting its

61
00:04:05.108 --> 00:04:09.348
requirements. Trace the dates and the particular provision before deciding.

62
00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:11.970
Finish with a separate claim example.

63
00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.870
The issued policy has a one hundred thousand dollar death benefit,

64
00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:20.970
a twelve thousand dollar outstanding loan and six hundred dollars of

65
00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:25.810
accrued loan interest. Assume a covered death, no other benefit additions

66
00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:27.590
and no other deductions.

67
00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.610
The net payment is eighty-seven thousand four hundred dollars.

68
00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:36.490
Do not add the cash value to the face amount unless

69
00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:39.030
the actual contract provides that result.

70
00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:43.510
The agent should help the beneficiary follow the insurer's claim process

71
00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:47.990
and keep clear records, while the insurer determines the payment under

72
00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:52.970
the contract. Good exam answers follow the stated person, event,

73
00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:55.070
provision and calculation.
